WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.220
THE MIDDLE CLASS:
I’m sure you’ve heard about the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:02.220 --> 00:00:07.800
middle class shrinking. Growing wealth inequality,&nbsp;
housing unaffordability, less retirement savings,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:07.800 --> 00:00:13.440
inaccessible healthcare, and declining purchasing&nbsp;
power. These have become the hallmarks of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:13.440 --> 00:00:19.020
rapidly disappearing middle class which has&nbsp;
shrunk over 10% within the past 50 years.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:19.680 --> 00:00:25.680
That might not sound like a big number but keep&nbsp;
in mind, that refers to over 30 million people.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:25.680 --> 00:00:33.000
Not to mention, many of the people who are still&nbsp;
part of the middle class are just scraping by. The&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:33.000 --> 00:00:38.280
mainstream media frequently attributes this shift&nbsp;
to capitalism, billionaires, inflation, politics,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:38.280 --> 00:00:43.920
and greed. There's no arguing that these factors&nbsp;
have indeed played a role in the growing wealth&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:43.920 --> 00:00:49.320
inequality, but what if I told you that there&nbsp;
was an X factor that no one ever talks about:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:49.980 --> 00:00:55.980
tech. Tech is often seen in the exact&nbsp;
opposite light as the great equalizer.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:55.980 --> 00:01:00.540
It’s what gives everyone access to the same&nbsp;
information, knowledge, and opportunities.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:00.540 --> 00:01:05.580
But rarely do people look at the other side&nbsp;
of this equation. Take a look at this graph&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:05.580 --> 00:01:11.640
for example. It portrays the rise tech over the&nbsp;
past 50 years. Now, compare it to the graph about&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:11.640 --> 00:01:18.120
the shrinking middle class. You’ll see that it’s&nbsp;
almost a perfect inverse relationship. Of course,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:18.120 --> 00:01:24.960
correlation does not imply causation but thinking&nbsp;
more about it, this would make a whole lot of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:24.960 --> 00:01:27.536
sense. On a macro scale, tech has made the global&nbsp;
economy far more competitive as far more people&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:27.536 --> 00:01:28.200
are able to participate and compete. On&nbsp;
a micro scale, tech has made traditional&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:28.200 --> 00:01:33.540
jobs like being a teacher, police officer,&nbsp;
and firefighter completely unviable as the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:33.540 --> 00:01:39.840
cost of living in many areas has adapted to&nbsp;
tech compensation. So, is this really just&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:39.840 --> 00:01:45.600
a correlation or is tech the culprit that’s been&nbsp;
secretly destroying the middle class for decades?

00:01:45.600 --> 00:01:49.620
THE SPECULATIVE ERA:&nbsp;

00:01:49.620 --> 00:02:01.260
Taking a look back, the impact of tech on&nbsp;
economics can be traced all the way back to the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:01.260 --> 00:02:07.560
1950s. This was the beginning of modern tech as we&nbsp;
saw the formation of the silicon wafer industry.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:08.100 --> 00:02:13.740
At the time, the tech industry was really quite&nbsp;
a foreign topic to the general public. It was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:13.740 --> 00:02:18.180
just something that super smart researchers at&nbsp;
prestigious universities messed with, similar&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:18.180 --> 00:02:23.700
to quantum computing today. There was really no&nbsp;
commercial applications for tech and as such,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:23.700 --> 00:02:29.520
there was really no money in tech either. It&nbsp;
was all funded by meager government grants and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:29.520 --> 00:02:34.920
university budgets. So, the industry was really&nbsp;
just filled with a bunch of people who were truly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:34.920 --> 00:02:41.820
passionate about tech but all of this would change&nbsp;
in 1957. 8 PhD graduates who were conducting&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:41.820 --> 00:02:46.440
research under professor William Shockley would&nbsp;
take his research and start their own company:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:46.440 --> 00:02:53.040
Fairchild Semiconductor. This betrayal would&nbsp;
make way for the rise of the entire semiconductor&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:53.040 --> 00:02:58.440
industry as it led to the creation of dozens&nbsp;
of tech companies including both Intel and AMD.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:58.440 --> 00:03:05.580
This made tech a relatively viable business by&nbsp;
the 1970s but it still didn’t have all that big&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:05.580 --> 00:03:11.580
of an impact on the economy. Most people just&nbsp;
viewed it as a fad or as a niche industry. A&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:11.580 --> 00:03:16.260
perfect example showcasing this mindset is Steve&nbsp;
Ballmer’s parents. If you’re not familiar with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:16.260 --> 00:03:23.040
Steve Ballmer, he was the former CEO of Microsoft&nbsp;
and is worth over $100 billion. But back in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:23.040 --> 00:03:28.200
1970s when he told his parents that he was gonna&nbsp;
drop out of Stanford to join Bill Gates, his dad&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:28.200 --> 00:03:33.720
said “What the heck is software?” and his mom&nbsp;
said “Why would a person ever need a computer?”&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:34.380 --> 00:03:39.900
Let’s just say those statements aged really&nbsp;
well but you can’t even blame them as tech&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:39.900 --> 00:03:45.000
was seen in the same light as crypto is today.&nbsp;
Some people swear by it claiming that it’s the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:45.000 --> 00:03:50.820
future of money, finance, and economic growth.&nbsp;
Most, however, wouldn’t put much more than 10&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:50.820 --> 00:03:56.340
bucks in Bitcoin. But it wasn’t long until the&nbsp;
people who bet on tech early would start seeing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:56.340 --> 00:04:03.840
massive upside starting with Steve Jobs. In 1978&nbsp;
Steve Jobs would make his first million at age&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:03.840 --> 00:04:11.640
23. Bill Gates would follow just a few years later&nbsp;
in 1981. But, while this was impressive, it didn’t&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:11.640 --> 00:04:17.100
mean all that much. Virtually all of their net&nbsp;
worth was just on paper. This however would all&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:17.100 --> 00:04:23.880
change on December 12, 1980. This was the day that&nbsp;
Apple went public giving Steve Jobs a net worth of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:23.880 --> 00:04:31.560
$217 million. This too was completely on paper&nbsp;
but as a public company, the legitimacy of his&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:31.560 --> 00:04:36.840
wealth and tech wealth in general skyrocketed.&nbsp;
6 years later, Bill Gates would take it the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:36.840 --> 00:04:43.380
next level with his own IPO that gave him a net&nbsp;
worth of $350 million. Just one year after this,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:43.380 --> 00:04:49.560
Bill Gates would not just become a billionaire but&nbsp;
the world’s youngest billionaire. It was official,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:49.560 --> 00:04:56.400
you could make serious money off of tech but&nbsp;
tech was still very much a niche portion of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:56.400 --> 00:05:01.740
economy. It was something that young nerds who&nbsp;
have nothing to lose like Bill or Steve messed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:01.740 --> 00:05:07.560
with. Everyone else though had real jobs if you&nbsp;
know what I mean. Fortunately for them, ignoring&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:07.560 --> 00:05:13.260
tech didn’t really affect them all that much but&nbsp;
this would slowly start to change with the 1990s.

00:05:19.740 --> 00:05:29.580
THE OPPORTUNITY ERA:
The 1990s was characterized by an explosion in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:29.580 --> 00:05:35.040
the number of tech companies. The general public&nbsp;
still hadn’t bought into tech but investors had.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:35.040 --> 00:05:40.560
As such, anyone and everyone could get funding&nbsp;
to go try their luck at making it big in the tech&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:40.560 --> 00:05:45.180
world. And many did, hundreds of successful tech&nbsp;
companies were started during this time period&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:45.180 --> 00:05:50.640
propelling their founders into the billions. But&nbsp;
that’s not what we’re really concerned about.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:50.640 --> 00:05:56.400
We care more about the tech industry’s impact on&nbsp;
middle class and this was really the first middle&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:56.400 --> 00:06:02.700
class generation that got involved in tech. In the&nbsp;
late 80s and early 90s, an unprecedented number of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:02.700 --> 00:06:08.400
people would start majoring in computer science.&nbsp;
Over 40,000 people were getting CS degrees every&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:08.400 --> 00:06:14.640
single year creating a new sector of the middle&nbsp;
class. Things didn’t get too out of hand though.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:14.640 --> 00:06:20.280
While tech companies paid generously, in the&nbsp;
grand scheme of things, compensation was still&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:20.280 --> 00:06:26.340
relatively modest. In 1995 for example, the&nbsp;
average Microsoft employee was making just&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:26.340 --> 00:06:33.600
under $60k. For perspective, the median household&nbsp;
income in 1995 was $37,000. So tech households&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:33.600 --> 00:06:39.720
were making about 50% more but nothing too crazy.&nbsp;
From time to time, you would hear about some tech&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:39.720 --> 00:06:44.520
employee that’s a friend of a friend that become&nbsp;
a millionaire thanks to stock compensation. But&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:44.520 --> 00:06:50.400
this was more of a gamble than anything especially&nbsp;
during the dotcom bubble when the vast majority&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:50.400 --> 00:06:56.460
of internet companies went under. Speaking of the&nbsp;
dotcom bubble, the burst would completely destroy&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:56.460 --> 00:07:03.180
the tech spirit. Annual CS grads would fall from&nbsp;
a peak of 60,000 per year back down to 40,000 as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:03.180 --> 00:07:09.480
people feared that tech was indeed just a fad. I&nbsp;
can imagine the old folks back in the day telling&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:09.480 --> 00:07:15.060
their unemployed CS children that they told them&nbsp;
so. They should have just become an engineer or&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:15.060 --> 00:07:19.620
a government employee. While they wouldn’t have&nbsp;
the same earnings potential, they would’ve had&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:19.620 --> 00:07:25.380
far more stability and peace of mind. This was&nbsp;
the state of tech for much of the 2000s. While&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:25.380 --> 00:07:28.740
there were a few startups here and there like&nbsp;
Google and Facebook that were making strides,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:28.740 --> 00:07:35.100
the big tech industry had fallen and it had&nbsp;
fallen hard. In fact, many of these tech giants&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:35.100 --> 00:07:41.100
would never recover. Cisco for example, was at one&nbsp;
point the world’s largest company. But during the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:41.100 --> 00:07:46.860
dotcom crash, they would fall 80% which they&nbsp;
still haven’t recovered from. It was the same&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:46.860 --> 00:07:53.280
thing with Intel and AT&amp;T and Xerox and basically&nbsp;
every other big tech giant of the era. Microsoft&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:53.280 --> 00:07:59.580
was the only giant that ended up recovering but&nbsp;
it took them 17 years and a fundamental shift to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:59.580 --> 00:08:05.100
do so. It seemed like the tech prognosis&nbsp;
was pretty clear. Tech was here to stay,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:05.100 --> 00:08:11.220
sure but it’s days of exponential growth were&nbsp;
over. Looking forward, it seemed like tech would&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:11.220 --> 00:08:16.860
just become another established industry like&nbsp;
finance, healthcare, or education. People would of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:16.860 --> 00:08:22.020
course continue pursuing CS degrees but it wasn’t&nbsp;
in the hopes of becoming an overnight millionaire.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:22.020 --> 00:08:28.440
The perspective was much more pragmatic. Tech&nbsp;
simply paid more than most other industries and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:28.440 --> 00:08:33.660
these big giants like Cisco and Microsoft were&nbsp;
much more relaible than the startups of the 80s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:33.660 --> 00:08:40.629
and 90s. This created a new upper middle class,&nbsp;
the tech class that were just slightly more well&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:40.629 --> 00:08:46.980
off than the middle class. This divide, however,&nbsp;
was about to grow to indescribable proportions.

00:08:51.180 --> 00:08:58.680
LEFT BEHIND:
It’s no secret that tech compensation has&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:58.680 --> 00:09:03.900
gone through the roof over the past 15 years.&nbsp;
Google’s median compensation currently stands at&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:04.980 --> 00:09:10.260
$295,000. Comparing this to Microsoft’s average&nbsp;
compensation 30 years ago, we’re talking about&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:10.260 --> 00:09:16.920
over a 5X difference. And this disparity becomes&nbsp;
even larger when we compare to household income&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:16.920 --> 00:09:23.220
growth. Over the past 30 years, median household&nbsp;
income hasn’t even doubled while tech compensation&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:23.220 --> 00:09:28.800
has over quintupled. But that’s not even that&nbsp;
accurate of a way to look at it because the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:28.800 --> 00:09:34.320
average household is just earning cash while tech&nbsp;
households are earning tech stock which over the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:34.320 --> 00:09:41.460
past 15 years has been 5 to 10 times more valuable&nbsp;
than cash. So a $300,000 compensation package&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:41.460 --> 00:09:48.000
from Google is actually closer to earning $700 or&nbsp;
$800,000 in cash somewhere else. In other words,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:48.000 --> 00:09:54.360
back in the 1990s, tech people at the top&nbsp;
tech companies were earning 40-50% more than&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:54.360 --> 00:10:00.600
the middle class. Today, tech people at the&nbsp;
top tech companies are earning 10 times more.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:00.600 --> 00:10:07.380
We should also mention that the term “top tech&nbsp;
people” encompasses far more people. Back in 1995,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:07.380 --> 00:10:16.620
Microsoft themselves only had 18,000 employees.&nbsp;
Today, Microsoft has 221,000, Google has 174,000,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:16.620 --> 00:10:23.340
Apple has 164,000, Meta has 71,000, and those&nbsp;
are just the mega tech companies. Zooming out,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:23.340 --> 00:10:28.920
we’re talking about tens of millions of people&nbsp;
who are earning leaps and bounds more than any&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:28.920 --> 00:10:35.700
other profession. And I think this graph really&nbsp;
encapsulates exactly what I’m talking about. Tech&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:35.700 --> 00:10:42.900
has objectively eclipsed every other sector&nbsp;
by nearly 10X and that was as of 2015. Also,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:42.900 --> 00:10:47.760
I’m not just talking about people who are directly&nbsp;
employed by these tech companies or even people&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:47.760 --> 00:10:53.400
who are working in tech specifically. I’m talking&nbsp;
about people who leveraged tech vs people who&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:53.400 --> 00:10:59.040
didn’t. Uber, just a regular old taxi company&nbsp;
but combined with the power of tech, they’re&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:59.040 --> 00:11:05.280
worth nearly $100 billion. AirBnB, just a regular&nbsp;
old rental company but combined with the power of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:05.280 --> 00:11:11.220
tech, they’re worth over $80 billion. Similarly,&nbsp;
law firms are earning $90 million for just a few&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:11.220 --> 00:11:16.140
months of work for a tech company. Teachers are&nbsp;
earning millions for teaching online instead of at&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:16.140 --> 00:11:22.080
public schools. And the top influencers are worth&nbsp;
hundreds of millions if not billions. And those&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:22.080 --> 00:11:28.080
are just a few examples. Tech has seeped into&nbsp;
every industry and sector you can think of and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:28.080 --> 00:11:35.280
the wealthiest people of any industry are the ones&nbsp;
leveraging and investing in tech. Everyone else is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:35.280 --> 00:11:41.760
unfortunately stuck at $40 to $60k jobs hoping&nbsp;
for a 3% raise every year. When tech was only 1&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:41.760 --> 00:11:48.180
to 5% of the population, this wasn’t too big of&nbsp;
a deal. But as tech has grown to encompass 20,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:48.180 --> 00:11:54.300
30, 40% of the population and in some cities&nbsp;
even the majority, we have started to see a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:54.300 --> 00:12:00.840
massive wealth divide. And it’s not caused&nbsp;
by the top 1% or low taxes or capitalism&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:00.840 --> 00:12:07.020
or any of that. It’s actually just caused by&nbsp;
our peers who made in tech. Let me explain.

00:12:12.140 --> 00:12:13.140
THE&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:13.140 --> 00:12:14.160
CONSEQUENCES:&nbsp;

00:12:14.160 --> 00:12:23.880
It’s easy and quite popular to blame some sort&nbsp;
of elite class or Blackrock for the pains of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:23.880 --> 00:12:29.940
majority but really that’s not the case. Jeff&nbsp;
Bezos spending $500 million on a yacht isn’t&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:29.940 --> 00:12:35.460
causing your groceries and rent to skyrocket. Do&nbsp;
you know what it is though? The people around you.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:36.180 --> 00:12:41.340
When someone around you is willing to pay more&nbsp;
for groceries or houses or cars or whatever it be,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:41.340 --> 00:12:48.000
prices are naturally going to go up, and the best&nbsp;
example of this is Austin. Austin has always been&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:48.000 --> 00:12:53.820
part of the tech scene as its home to Dell. But&nbsp;
over the past 10 years and especially during the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:53.820 --> 00:12:59.280
pandemic, every tech company you can think of has&nbsp;
expanded to Austin or has grown their presence in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:59.280 --> 00:13:04.260
Austin. I mean just take a look at the companies&nbsp;
with the largest office space in Austin. Number&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:04.260 --> 00:13:11.220
1 is of course Dell, but then you have Apple,&nbsp;
Google, IBM, Oracle, Indeed, and Meta. As such,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:11.220 --> 00:13:17.880
Austin has seen an insane influx of high paid tech&nbsp;
workers from California and Seattle. Consequently,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:17.880 --> 00:13:23.220
the median price of houses has nearly tripled,&nbsp;
rent has gone up over 13% annually, groceries have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:23.220 --> 00:13:29.100
gone up 14% annually, and basically everything has&nbsp;
become far less affordable. I’ve lived in Austin&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:29.100 --> 00:13:34.680
for nearly my whole life and over the past few&nbsp;
years I’ve seen countless neighborhoods pop up&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:34.680 --> 00:13:41.160
where literally every single person works a remote&nbsp;
tech job and owns a Tesla. And I’m quite confident&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:41.160 --> 00:13:47.400
that these people are the number 1 reason for&nbsp;
skyrocketing prices in such cities. This isn’t to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:47.400 --> 00:13:52.920
say that we should blame these people or that it’s&nbsp;
their fault by any means though. Like anyone else,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:52.920 --> 00:13:58.500
they’re just trying to create the best lives for&nbsp;
themselves. With that being said though, it’s very&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:58.500 --> 00:14:04.680
important that we understand the modern economy&nbsp;
for what it is. It’s not a case of the top 1% vs&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:04.680 --> 00:14:10.260
everyone else. It’s a case of people in tech and&nbsp;
it’s surrounding sectors vs people who are not.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:10.260 --> 00:14:17.400
The modern economy works for tech and it sucks for&nbsp;
everyone else. This has been a growing trend for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:17.400 --> 00:14:23.520
many decades but we’re currently at a extreme&nbsp;
crossroads where the options are basically to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:23.520 --> 00:14:30.120
join or die. And this makes sense because moving&nbsp;
forward, everything will be tech related and we’re&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:30.120 --> 00:14:35.640
currently going through the painful transition to&nbsp;
where tech becomes the majority. So, in the end,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:35.640 --> 00:14:41.160
I don't think the current economic pain is due to&nbsp;
money printing or high inflation or bad politics&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:41.160 --> 00:14:46.980
or any of that, though they all play a role for&nbsp;
sure. Rather, I believe the current economic pain&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:46.980 --> 00:14:53.820
is a byproduct of the world economy evolving into&nbsp;
one where tech is not just a leading voice but the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:53.820 --> 00:14:59.700
dominant voice. If you’re already part of the tech&nbsp;
world, you’ll have a great time. If you’re not,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:59.700 --> 00:15:05.460
the future won’t be all that different from a&nbsp;
tech dystopia and that’s what’s really happening&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:05.460 --> 00:15:11.160
to the middle class. Speaking of the modern tech&nbsp;
economy, a lot of people are worried that AI is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:11.160 --> 00:15:16.020
gonna take everyone’s jobs. Check out this&nbsp;
video to hear why that won’t be the case.

